Mutual exclusion w/o hw CAS support.

===========
Background:
===========
Consider the typical example of cpu and pmu. Both can access each others memory but not do atomic RMW.

So a single lock variable will not work because you cannot atomically (test if hes using - if not - let me set it)


===========
Petersons:
===========
The solution is simple. Instead of one lock have two INTENTION_LOCK variables one in each entities' memory space. In addition also have
a TURN variable.


LOCK:
	Self->INTENTION_LOCK = true;
	TURN = Other;

	while (Other->INTENTION_LOCK && (TURN == Other)) {}


UNLOCK:
	Self->INTENTION_LOCK = false;


	
===========
Analysis:
===========
As you will notice in addition to mutual exclusion w/o any hw support it also has the advantage of being wait-free. Why?
Because the  max hop for a thread when it decides it needs the lock to the time it gets the lock is 1 lost LOCK attempt.

In other words one thread cannot acquire the same lock back to back *AFTER* the other thread has done INTENTION_LOCK == true
(check for yourself)


===========
Bottomline:
===========
Format of the algorithm is :

=> DECLARE INTENTION TO LOCK

=> GIVE THE OTHER THREAD THE TURN

=> WAIT WHILE THE OTHER THREAD'S INTENTION *AND* TURN HOLD TRUE
